WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:04.170
Rodger: My name's Rodger Cornell.

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:05.520
I'm an explosives engineer.

00:00:10.154 --> 00:00:11.790
Will Ferrell: Oh my god,
how do they walk away

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:14.811
in movies without flinching
when it explodes behind them?

00:00:14.811 --> 00:00:16.142
There's no way!

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.150
Rodger: Okay so as soon
as this clip starts

00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:29.770
there are a couple of
really pressing issues

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:31.350
that you should be focusing on.

00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:33.030
The first is if you have grenades

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.660
and other weapons, you
shouldn't be carrying them

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:36.913
around inside a guitar case.

00:00:38.279 --> 00:00:39.910
On top of that, as soon as
he pulls the grenades out

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:41.870
you'll notice that he pulls out not one

00:00:41.870 --> 00:00:43.813
but two of those pins with his teeth.

00:00:45.320 --> 00:00:46.840
I've tried it a few
times, and you really have

00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.430
to put all of your strength
to get that thing dislodged.

00:00:49.430 --> 00:00:51.900
So pulling any kind of grenade
pin out with your mouth

00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.290
unless it's been tampered with beforehand

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:56.432
is just completely unrealistic.

00:00:56.432 --> 00:00:57.610
Antonio Banderas: Jesus Christ.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:00:59.991
Rodger: The most common
misconceptions I'm aware of.

00:00:59.991 --> 00:01:02.360
One is that any explosive device

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.680
when it goes off will throw a human being

00:01:04.680 --> 00:01:06.600
into the air. The detonation itself

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.680
and the pressure wave,
they're gonna do plenty

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:10.040
of damage to the human body.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:11.750
Propelling you into the air isn't gonna

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.570
be one of the things that you experience.

00:01:13.570 --> 00:01:17.003
So being thrown is not really a thing.

00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.110
You also see that just
from those two grenades,

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:30.370
you have this gigantic fireball.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:32.610
Grenades, especially a
military grenade, is gonna

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:35.630
be composed of what's
called secondary explosives

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:37.020
and these secondary explosives go

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:38.850
through detonation processes.

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:41.260
A detonation process is extremely rapid

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:42.230
and it's certainly not gonna

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:46.063
be this prolonged explosion
that you see here.

00:01:57.100 --> 00:01:58.550
When you see a gigantic fireball

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:00.320
and you see just flames everywhere

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.980
and you can usually assume

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.860
that they're exaggerating quite a bit.

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:05.070
I mean it's not that surprising.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:06.963
It's a Quentin Tarantino movie,

00:02:06.963 --> 00:02:09.430
so I really wouldn't expect anything less.

00:02:09.430 --> 00:02:11.740
The only explosive device that you see

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:15.040
in that clip is what seems
to be one pack of dynamite.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:18.320
Dynamite's used specifically
for blasting operations

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:22.840
so even though it's made
for situations like this

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:24.570
if you have just one bundle in the corner

00:02:24.570 --> 00:02:27.930
of a house, you will never
get any kind of an explosion

00:02:27.930 --> 00:02:31.110
on this order, you might have
a chunk of the house missing

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:32.680
right where that explosive charge was

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.420
but that's about it,
the house would probably

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.180
still be standing, unless there were a lot

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.940
of other charges placed about the house

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:39.050
that we're unaware of.

00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:40.190
Certainly not every piece

00:02:40.190 --> 00:02:42.262
of the building would be in flames.

00:02:42.262 --> 00:02:44.429
Samuel Jackson: Son of a--

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.390
It looks like he's in a dummy town.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:59.030
Once he hears sirens, he realizes

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.370
that he's about to be in the
middle of a nuclear test.

00:03:01.370 --> 00:03:03.611
Harrison Ford: That can't be good.

00:03:08.117 --> 00:03:09.700
Rodger: The refrigerator's lead lined

00:03:09.700 --> 00:03:11.070
and the reason this is important is

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:13.850
that the lead can actually
block certain types

00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:15.760
of radiation. So that's the premise

00:03:15.760 --> 00:03:17.410
is that all the lead
in this fridge is gonna

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:21.440
block the radiation and
somehow protect Indiana Jones.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.200
Some of the things that are
depicting are somewhat accurate.

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:26.642
I mean, a nuclear detonation
or a nuclear event

00:03:26.642 --> 00:03:28.763
would produce all kinds of light.

00:03:30.820 --> 00:03:33.063
And it's amazingly destructive.

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:37.260
So they're showing the
force of it correctly

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:39.250
just not how it's gonna impact the fridge

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:40.950
which is the main issue with the scene.

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.280
Sure, the lead lining is
gonna protect a little bit

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:45.950
from some of the radiation,
although it's not nearly enough

00:03:45.950 --> 00:03:47.490
to prevent all of the radiation

00:03:47.490 --> 00:03:49.910
from affecting you, there's
no way you can survive

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:51.020
an impact like that.

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:52.690
The fact that he gets out immediately

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:54.330
and he'll actually walk up to look

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:56.820
at the aftermath of the mushroom cloud

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.750
from the nuclear event, there's
a ton of nuclear fallout

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:01.970
when you have these events, and you want

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:03.560
to be as far away from this as possible

00:04:03.560 --> 00:04:05.680
because the radiation that it produces

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:07.980
it sticks around for quite a while.

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.540
And the fact that he lives on in the movie

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:10.960
and doesn't die in a couple hours

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.423
from radiation sickness is
just completely ridiculous.

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.764
Right off the bat

00:04:28.764 --> 00:04:31.900
it's Michael Bay, it's
a Transformers movie

00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:34.170
you have Deceptacons running around.

00:04:34.170 --> 00:04:36.860
So how real can you expect it to be?

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:38.660
When you see the fighter jets in action

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:42.943
you'll notice these bursts of
light coming off the bottom.

00:04:44.010 --> 00:04:45.130
If I had to guess what that was,

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:46.770
that would probably be flares.

00:04:46.770 --> 00:04:48.720
And a lot of times
they're used so the planes

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.620
can avoid heat seeking missiles.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:53.170
They're a source of heat
that can distract the missile

00:04:53.170 --> 00:04:55.170
from continuing to fly towards the jet.

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:57.620
Now whether or not the Deceptacons

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:00.570
use heat-seeking missiles I have no idea.

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:01.613
Traffic Controller: Q1 snipe 090.

00:05:01.613 --> 00:05:04.160
You are approved to drop
your 2000-class JDAMs.

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:06.448
Rodger: So during this initial explosion,

00:05:06.448 --> 00:05:09.460
what I'm assuming they're
doing is essentially

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:10.760
marking their target.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:12.760
If you're gonna drop ammunition

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:14.700
that don't have any kind of propulsion

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:17.550
or guidance, you want
to be very, very sure

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:19.813
where you're gonna drop those bombs.

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:31.280
You see Shia LaBeouf get
just almost catapulted

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:33.460
into the air, it almost
actually looks like he's

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:35.193
stepping on like a trampoline.

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:39.910
I can't see any reason why he would

00:05:39.910 --> 00:05:41.930
be tumbling in the air like that.

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:44.150
The pressure wave that's radiating

00:05:44.150 --> 00:05:48.060
is moving outward, away from the explosion

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:50.395
so if you were to be thrown at all

00:05:50.395 --> 00:05:52.960
you might be thrown backwards
and fall to the ground

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:54.410
but setting that all aside,

00:05:54.410 --> 00:05:56.650
there's at least realistic situations.

00:05:56.650 --> 00:05:59.650
Some of the things that the aircraft did

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:02.310
that progression from marking your target

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:04.930
and then dropping the
energetic load on top of it

00:06:04.930 --> 00:06:06.610
my guess is that he was probably consulted

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:09.930
as to how the Department
of Defense would actually

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:11.460
go through their procedures.

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:26.180
He takes essentially his claws

00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:27.630
and he strikes them against the rocks

00:06:27.630 --> 00:06:30.240
to create a spark to initiate the flame.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.330
If I'm remembering
correctly, it's Adamantium

00:06:32.330 --> 00:06:35.550
what his claws are made of,
which is completely fictitious.

00:06:35.550 --> 00:06:36.840
I have no ideas what the properties of

00:06:36.840 --> 00:06:38.960
that material would be, but
there are a lot of metals

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:40.710
out there that don't spark.

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:43.010
There are other ways to initiate fuel.

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:44.360
That seems like a pretty silly one

00:06:44.360 --> 00:06:45.980
that probably wouldn't work out.

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:48.000
That's a very rocky surface.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:49.690
Even if you got it to start burning

00:06:49.690 --> 00:06:50.610
it would probably die out

00:06:50.610 --> 00:06:52.150
before it ever reached the helicopter.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:54.540
I would never expect this to explode.

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:56.817
You have a helicopter that's crashed

00:06:56.817 --> 00:06:58.840
and that's already leaking fuel.

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:00.520
So when the fuel tank ruptures

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:03.040
there's no pressure
vessel that can heat up

00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:04.670
that can allow pressure to build

00:07:04.670 --> 00:07:06.880
where you'd actually get
some sort of an explosion.

00:07:06.880 --> 00:07:09.550
But if you had an explosion on that scale

00:07:09.550 --> 00:07:12.090
you'd have pieces of that
helicopter being thrown

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:14.670
in every direction, not to
mention the pressure wave

00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:17.300
that's produced, so he would definitely

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:18.830
hit the ground at some point.

00:07:18.830 --> 00:07:21.550
He regenerates, so I guess
you could walk away from this.

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:22.383
He'd be fine.

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:37.663
You see some grenade
being launched at Hancock.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:43.640
And he somehow deflects it into a car.

00:07:43.640 --> 00:07:45.420
If we just accept the
fact that he can't be hurt

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:46.560
and that he can just deflect bullets

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:49.080
and that's fine, there's
still really big issues

00:07:49.080 --> 00:07:51.960
with this scene. The effect of the grenade

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:55.110
is unrealistic, the way
it initiates is incorrect

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:58.280
using the police car as a way

00:07:58.280 --> 00:08:02.060
of stopping bullets is just ill advised.

00:08:02.060 --> 00:08:04.250
Cars don't stop bullets.

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:05.970
Cars can deflect bullets.

00:08:05.970 --> 00:08:07.810
Cars have a lot of weak points,

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:10.090
so you wouldn't want to
ever use it as a shield.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:12.738
I would expect the doors to
have bullet-proof inserts

00:08:12.738 --> 00:08:14.380
but that's about it.

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:17.090
The roof of the car and
the floor of the car

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:19.560
are probably pretty thin, those are gonna

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:21.860
be the most vulnerable
parts of that vehicle.

00:08:21.860 --> 00:08:24.513
The way he's holding it is
probably the least safe way

00:08:24.513 --> 00:08:27.620
that he could be using
that car for protection.

00:08:27.620 --> 00:08:28.683
Will Smith: Good job!

00:08:40.694 --> 00:08:43.100
To create an explosion that massive

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:45.480
you would need more explosive material

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:47.090
than you could carry with you

00:08:47.090 --> 00:08:49.610
and you would need to plant it everywhere.

00:08:49.610 --> 00:08:53.280
It would take just unlimited
financial resources

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:55.540
a huge amount of time to place the charges

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:56.580
where you need to put them.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:59.833
They try to add in some
cool sound effects.

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:05.970
It's not gonna sound anything like that.

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:10.970
The actual audio event is very rapid

00:09:11.020 --> 00:09:13.863
very loud, and it's more of a crack.

00:09:17.210 --> 00:09:21.130
They try to show the shock
actually moving outward

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:23.750
essentially past Denzel,
you can see the grass

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:27.550
start to move forward,
and then retract back.

00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:29.503
There, you see that little.

00:09:29.503 --> 00:09:31.590
That was a shock wave moving past,

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:34.220
so seeing that sort of
motion within the grass

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:37.080
in that scene could actually be realistic.

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:39.110
If you're that close where
you can see the shock

00:09:39.110 --> 00:09:42.370
around you, it's gonna at least hurt

00:09:42.370 --> 00:09:45.250
because you have this
sudden change in pressure.

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:47.070
It's gonna feel like
someone's at the very least

00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.730
just punching you as hard as they can.

00:09:49.730 --> 00:09:51.070
You're not just gonna keep walking.

00:09:51.070 --> 00:09:53.200
All sorts of metal being thrown.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:54.850
There's fragmentation everywhere.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:12.350
This is one of my favorite movies

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:13.290
so I really hate

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:15.980
laying into it like I'm about to.

00:10:17.126 --> 00:10:18.800
So he essentially rigged this hospital

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:22.230
with explosives, he's
setting them off wirelessly

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:26.170
and then he's just watching
as the demolition unfolds.

00:10:26.170 --> 00:10:29.090
You could have explosions
going off at different times

00:10:29.090 --> 00:10:30.670
and different locations

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:32.620
so that's actually not unreasonable.

00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:35.200
What is unreasonable is
to pull that off would

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:37.955
be expensive, and you'd
also need to somehow

00:10:37.955 --> 00:10:41.180
go through a hospital and
place all these charges

00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:44.280
without anybody noticing,
I'm not entirely sure

00:10:44.280 --> 00:10:46.400
that he would have the resources

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:50.170
the time, and be able
to disguise everything

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:51.700
to actually pull something like this off.

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:54.343
Especially given what the Joker's wearing.

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:56.830
Heath Ledger: Hi.

00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:59.880
There's no way he would go
through this entire process

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:01.793
without being seen by somebody.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:07.193
Jonah Hill: It's gonna explode!

00:11:07.193 --> 00:11:08.026
Channing Tatum: Oh my
god, roll up the windows!

00:11:08.026 --> 00:11:08.968
Jonah: What the f*** is that?
Channing: Roll it up!

00:11:08.968 --> 00:11:10.301
Channing: I don't care just do it!

00:11:12.307 --> 00:11:14.480
- The funny thing is, this scene is one

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:17.200
of the most realistic scenes
we're probably gonna see.

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:20.740
There is one resounding
issue with this scene though.

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:22.670
All those pressurized tanks, the fact

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:24.010
that all these things are ruptured

00:11:24.010 --> 00:11:25.020
and just sitting there.

00:11:25.020 --> 00:11:26.510
Jonah: What happened?

00:11:26.510 --> 00:11:27.830
Channing: I don't know.
It just didn't explode.

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:28.663
Jonah: That's weird.

00:11:28.663 --> 00:11:29.642
That would never happen.

00:11:36.150 --> 00:11:38.060
Jonah: I really thought
that one was gonna explode.

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:40.580
This I think is actually
the most accurate scene

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:44.240
so you have a fuel tank
that was just riddled

00:11:44.240 --> 00:11:46.093
with bullet holes, so it has no way

00:11:46.093 --> 00:11:48.233
of accelerating the reaction.

00:11:55.588 --> 00:11:57.181
Channing: That's what exploded?

00:11:57.181 --> 00:11:58.980
I don't know why chickens would explode.

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:01.330
Their fecal matter could probably combust

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:03.703
the wooden cages could light on fire

00:12:03.703 --> 00:12:06.700
but why you'd get an
explosion, I have no idea.

00:12:06.700 --> 00:12:08.840
I think the scene's kind of intending

00:12:08.840 --> 00:12:12.563
to be a bit ridiculous,
and it definitely is.

00:12:18.970 --> 00:12:22.360
Will: What accounting firm is
closed at 11 AM on a Tuesday?

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:23.342
Mark Wahlberg: Oh this is a s***hole.

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:29.016
The flame's unrealistic, but
the way they're pushed back

00:12:29.016 --> 00:12:31.020
is actually somewhat believable.

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:33.710
So the fact that they aren't cut up

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:35.460
and that they're not bleeding profusely.

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:37.420
The fragmentation in that situation

00:12:37.420 --> 00:12:38.960
would be much more deadly.

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:40.579
Will: I can't hear!

00:12:40.579 --> 00:12:41.950
I can't hear!

00:12:41.950 --> 00:12:44.520
The way he's complaining is unrealistic

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:45.580
but the fact that he's complaining

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:48.410
about his ears is actually realistic.

00:12:48.410 --> 00:12:50.190
When you have blast over pressure

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:52.190
there are certain parts of the human body

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:54.860
that are susceptible, are very susceptible

00:12:54.860 --> 00:12:56.270
to shifts in pressure.

00:12:56.270 --> 00:12:59.780
Your ear drums are one,
and your lungs are another.

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:01.790
In extreme cases, if the
pressure was strong enough

00:13:01.790 --> 00:13:05.090
you could rupture your
lungs, and if that happens

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:06.800
or if anything near that happens

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:08.350
he's not gonna be talking.

00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:09.970
He's just gonna be laying there gasping

00:13:09.970 --> 00:13:11.960
for whatever breath he can pull in

00:13:11.960 --> 00:13:13.660
so he seems to be in much better shape

00:13:13.660 --> 00:13:15.090
than I would imagine him to be.

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:16.550
If you got hit with a pressure wave

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:19.861
you'd probably be concussed as well.

00:13:19.861 --> 00:13:21.830
Will: I need an MRI!

00:13:21.830 --> 00:13:24.663
So an MRI might not be a bad idea.

